Date: Sun, 2 Aug 2026 07:30:57 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] camera: choose the viewfinder resolution, and remember it

A camera offers sizes it cannot always stream. Asked for one of those, the
pipeline answers 'Failed to start streaming: Resource busy' and the viewfinder
is dead - no picture, and no error the user can act on. Measured on a Fairphone
3, each size in its own process starting from idle so that nothing here is a
matter of reconfiguring in a hurry: of the 47 sizes that camera advertises,
everything below about 1.8 megapixels fails and everything above it works -
160x120, 320x240, 400x240, 640x480, 800x600, 1024x768, 1280x720, 1280x1024,
1400x1050 and 1680x1050 all refuse, while 1600x1200, 1920x1080 and the sensor's
own 4032x3024 stream.

Which ones work therefore cannot be derived, only tried. On first use the
viewfinder bisects the list - which is ordered by pixel count - for the
smallest size that streams: a size that fails reports it through the pipeline's
error, which is an answer rather than a breakdown, so the search moves on
instead of giving up the camera. Bisecting matters: walking up from the
smallest would have cost thirty pipeline restarts on the camera above, in front
of a user looking at a black screen, where six suffice.

The bisection assumes that a size which streams keeps streaming as it grows.
Where that does not hold the result is merely not the smallest one that works,
never a size that does not, because only a size seen to stream is settled on.

The winner is published as the preview-resolution property, and the application
binds that to a setting in both directions. The search therefore happens once
in the lifetime of an installation rather than at every start, and a size
chosen by hand in the preferences takes precedence. Choosing one that turns out
not to stream is not fatal either: the search simply runs again.

Smallest-first is deliberate. It is the cheapest to debayer in software and so
the fastest preview, and the picture is taken at the sensor's full resolution
regardless of what the viewfinder runs at.
